Let $\alpha = 45^\circ - \frac{\theta}{2}.$ Then, $\sin \alpha = \cos(45^\circ + \frac{\theta}{2})$ and $\cos \alpha = \sin(45^\circ + \frac{\theta}{2}).$ Substituting these values, we get:
\[\frac{OC}{\sin \alpha} = \frac{1}{\cos \alpha}\]
\[OC = \tan \alpha = \tan(45^\circ - \frac{\theta}{2})\]
\[OC = \frac{\tan 45^\circ - \tan \frac{\theta}{2}}{1 + \tan 45^\circ \tan \frac{\theta}{2}}\]
\[OC = \frac{1 - \tan \frac{\theta}{2}}{1 + \tan \frac{\theta}{2}}\]

